I don't know if it's a bug or just me not grasping the usage of the CancellationToken correctly, since I couldn't find an explanation about it. I'm trying to make a simple mail client, so to show messages in a folder using the `ImapService` I do the following:


    static CancellationTokenSource _messagesTaskCts;
    IMailFolder _sourceFolder;

    async Task<ObservableCollection<MailMessage>> GetMessages()
    {
        try { _messagesTaskCts?.Cancel(); }
        catch (ObjectDisposedException) { /* The implicated task already finished */ }
        
        using (_messagesTaskCts = new CancellationTokenSource())
        {
            var cToken = _messagesTaskCts.Token;
            var messages = new ObservableCollection<MailMessage>();

            await _sourceFolder.OpenAsync(FolderAccess.ReadOnly).ConfigureAwait(false);
            var uids = (await _sourceFolder.SearchAsync(SearchQuery.All, cToken));

            foreach (var uid in uids)
            {
                var msg = await _sourceFolder.GetMessageAsync(uid, cToken);
                messages.Add(new MailMessage(msg));
            }

            return messages;
        }
    }


However, if one folder is currently trying to retrieve its messages and I want to start retrieving messages from another folder (i.e. cancel current task and start new) I end up getting a `ServiceNotConnectedException.cs` telling me that the client has been disconnected.

I expected that cancelling the token source would simply terminate the operation (be it `_sourceFolder.SearchAsync` or `_sourceFolder.GetMessageAsync`), but maybe that's not how I'm supposed to use the token?

Using MailKit v1.14.2 from NuGet

comments.body:
The way that cancellation works is that if a command is already in progress, it has to drop the connection (there's no other way).

The convention for doing cancellation should probably look a bit like this from your perspective:

```csharp
try {
    client.DoSomethingAsync (..., cancellationToken);
} catch (OperationCanceledException) {
    if (!client.IsConnected) {
        Reconnect ();
    }
}
```

I should note that the `ImapEngine` always checks cancelation state *before* issuing the command as well, so it's possible that the connection is not dropped if the command hasn't been sent yet, so you should always check `client.IsConnected`.

